  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nSurface high pressure and height rises favor dry weather with\nseasonable temperature today through Wednesday.\n\nThe next upper trough will slowly cross the Ohio River Valley\nThursday through Friday morning and increase shower/thunderstorm\nchances. Models suggest that the lack of a strong surface \nboundary and weaker jet ascent may limit the areal storm\ncoverage in what otherwise would be a more widespread, high\nprobability rain chance. Storms that do form should experience \nmodest CAPE (around 1000-1200 J/kg) and weak shear (around \n20kts), just enough to create non-zero severe hazard risk\n(mainly damaging wind). The best combination of these\nenvironmental factors and storm coverage is generally east of\nI-79, where SPC has highlighted a Marginal Risk in the Day 3\noutlook.\n\nLingering troughing (sfc/aloft) and moisture may foster a few\nisolated showers/thunderstorms Friday afternoon amid peak\nheating. Otherwise, surface high pressure building in from the\nNW and eventual height rises lend to drier weather plus\nseasonable temperature for Friday into the weekend. Ensemble\nmodel variability is larger to start the next week but \nscenarios exists severe-favoring upper level patterns worth\nmonitoring in the coming days.",
